body:
### What problem are you trying to solve?

`MutationObserver` is chunk-aligned rather than parser-aligned: when the HTML parser streams markup across network chunks, it inserts an element into the DOM on its opening tag and can yield before its closing tag arrives, flushing half-built subtrees to `MutationObserver` callbacks at arbitrary intermediate microtask checkpoints. By contrast, when DOM is built synchronously in script (`el.append(a); el.append(b);`), `MutationObserver` naturally batches all mutations in the task and delivers them together at the microtask checkpoint.

Components and streaming applications that need complete subtrees—such as custom elements that validate or index light-DOM children (`<s-select>` validating `<s-option>` children), elements that evaluate text/code once, or delegated island hydration—see incomplete children or perform redundant O(N^2) work across chunks.

At the same time, the central objection in WICG/webcomponents#809 to a dedicated one-shot custom element `parsedCallback()` is that it encourages reading `this.children` once at parse end and ignoring subsequent dynamic DOM mutations.

### What solutions exist today?

Because `MutationObserver` isn't aligned with element parse boundaries, server-driven and streaming applications reconstruct parse completion in userland at measurable main-thread and wire cost:

- **Ancestor `nextSibling` walk + per-element `subtree: true` `MutationObserver`** (`WebReflection/html-parsed-element`, `patricknelson/svelte-retag#20`): walks ancestors on every insertion (reported at 100ms+ of main-thread time on medium documents) and leaves last-child elements "unparsed" until `DOMContentLoaded`.
- **Regex over raw HTML chunks + `document.write()` into a detached document / `<iframe>`** (`marko-js/writable-dom`).
- **Sentinel child elements** (`<the-end>`) or **trailing inline `<script>` tags** after each streamed region (extra DOM nodes/wire bytes and requires CSP nonces/hashes or `unsafe-inline`).
- **Deferring `customElements.define()` to `DOMContentLoaded` or `requestAnimationFrame` / `setTimeout`**, which delays upgrades until the whole document finishes or races the parser across chunks.

### How would you solve it?

A synchronous `element.isParsing` getter (#12200), plus a way to align `MutationObserver` delivery with HTML parsing (without delaying DOM insertion itself—the parser still mutates the live DOM immediately). There are two candidate designs for the `MutationObserver` part.

```webidl
partial interface Element {
  readonly attribute boolean isParsing;
};
```

An element is parsing while it is on the HTML parser's stack of open elements (start tag inserted, end tag not yet popped), or while it is the context element of an active streaming fragment parser (`streamHTML*` or an active `<template for>` patch).

#### Option 1: parser-aligned delivery (`alignWithParser()`)

```webidl
partial interface MutationObserver {
  undefined alignWithParser(Element target, optional MutationObserverAlignOptions options = {});
};

dictionary MutationObserverAlignOptions {
  AbortSignal signal;
};
```

```js
class CustomSelect extends HTMLElement {
  #mo = new MutationObserver(() => this.#syncOptions());
  connectedCallback() {
    this.#mo.observe(this, { childList: true });
    this.#mo.alignWithParser(this);
    if (!this.isParsing) this.#syncOptions();
  }
}
```

1. **The whole observer is held (full delivery alignment):** While any target the observer is aligned with is parsing, the observer delivers nothing. When none is, its queue is delivered in one callback, in mutation order. `observe()` is unchanged, `subtree: true` works, and no option combination throws.
2. **Standing:** An alignment lasts until its `signal` is aborted or `disconnect()` is called, so later `streamHTML*()` / `<template for>` streams into `target` hold the observer again.
3. **Guaranteed callback when aligned during parsing:** If `target` is parsing when `alignWithParser()` is called, a flush is scheduled when parsing ends, invoking the callback with the held records or with an empty list. No record is synthesized. If `target` is not parsing, the call schedules nothing right now (the alignment still stands for future streams).

The main advantage is that the callback never has to check `isParsing`: it does not run while an aligned target is parsing. Because alignment belongs to the observer rather than to a registration, overlapping registrations (an ancestor observed with `subtree: true` and a descendant observed directly) cannot disagree about when to deliver a shared record.

The main trade-offs are changing microtask delivery timing (a held observer keeps its records across intermediate microtask checkpoints, including those after inline `<script>`s), needing one observer per element that completes independently, and handling already-parsed elements at the call site.

#### Option 2: parse-end `childList` (`parseEndChildList: true`)

With `element.isParsing` alone, a `childList` observer already receives a `childList` record at parse end whenever the final chunk added at least one child—the only gap is when the end tag closes an empty element or arrives in a chunk after the last child. `parseEndChildList: true` closes that gap so parse end always produces a `childList` record:

```webidl
partial dictionary MutationObserverInit {
  boolean parseEndChildList = false;
};
```

```js
class CustomSelect extends HTMLElement {
  #mo = new MutationObserver(() => {
    if (!this.isParsing) this.#syncOptions();
  });
  connectedCallback() {
    this.#mo.observe(this, { childList: true, parseEndChildList: true });
    if (!this.isParsing) this.#syncOptions();
  }
}
```

1. **No timing change:** Records are delivered at every microtask checkpoint as today. Nothing is held, and `observe()`, `takeRecords()`, and `disconnect()` behave as today.
2. **Guaranteed `childList` record on parse end:** When an element observed with `parseEndChildList: true` finishes parsing, if a `childList` record for it is already pending (because children arrived in the final chunk), that non-empty record covers parse end; only if none is pending is an empty fallback `childList` record (`addedNodes: []`, `removedNodes: []`) queued. Either way, the observer always gets a callback with a `childList` record after `isParsing` has become `false`.
3. **Target's own `childList` only:** Requires `childList: true`; combining with `subtree: true` throws a `TypeError`, so the parser does not have to report every element it closes.

This option leaves delivery timing untouched (and allows mixing `parseEndChildList: true` registrations with normal `subtree: true` registrations on one observer), and unlike a generic `parseEnd: true` name, states that parse end is surfaced through `childList` rather than a separate record type. The empty fallback record still requires relaxing the assertion in "queue a tree mutation record" that `addedNodes` or `removedNodes` is non-empty (Option 1 does not need this), and authors still get a callback per chunk, filter with `isParsing`, and handle already-parsed elements at the `observe()` call site.

**Off-main-thread / tree-op parsers (e.g. Gecko), both options:** An engine that separates parsing from main-thread DOM construction via queued tree operations does not need to emit an "end-parse" operation or flip an `isParsing` bit for every element's opening and closing tags (addressing the parser-to-DOM traffic concern in [w3c/csswg-drafts#12578 (comment)](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/12578#issuecomment-3209690246)). Any element that opens and closes without an intervening parser batch flush (chunk or timer boundary), `<script>`, or custom element reaction can never be observed by script while open—by the time any `MutationObserver` or script can hold a reference to it and read `el.isParsing`, it is already `false` (the default). The parser only needs to mark the $O(\text{stack depth})$ ancestor elements that are actually on the stack of open elements *at* a batch flush, `<script>`, or custom element reaction (and later emit a close op for those few nodes, which can itself be batched up to the next such boundary).

